E={12: '“And we see to it that your third mistake is your last; every time. The denial will chase you to the furthest corners of your sanity.” The leftmost titan adds unto the sayings of the other two titans.', 18: 'Yaldabaoth slithers past them, his shadow swallowing them whole, their souls consumed. He journeys past another moat, knowing he once fell into the first, though never having seen the second. He wonders how to accomplish the passage twice as long as the last, not understanding. For a moment, the God wonders, before casting a portal with his breath, and he yanks out the sun from the sky, casting the Earth in darkness. The Sun says unto the Yaldabaoth, “My will is to be let free!” The sun scorches his claws, and thus God drinks its flames, and the light grows weaker, almost putting out its own fire that’s left with sorrowful tears. “My God, your will is my own!” The Sun begs of the creator.', 20: '“My will is done then.” The Yaldabaoth throws the Sun into the abyssal moat, hoping to see, but nothing. The dark devours the light, and the sun almost sinks into the black tar. God, in a moment, is stricken in the fear of loss, and casts a noose at the Sun, trying to pull it out, but the quicksand of dark sinks the Sun, and pulls the demiurge down with it. God is pulled into the moat face first, his face unable to scream from the cover, and he thrashes in the soup. Before long, he calls upon the angels to save him, but they do not hear his desperation. He deathly rolls onto his back, like a child drowning in the pool of oil, screaming once more, “My angels! Obey my will!” As he says this, they cannot answer or act, for their wings were torn, their bones like broth, and their eyes and mouths sewn shut. He cries out once more, “Anyone!” Not even the titans can retrieve him, for he swallowed their souls. He cries in desperation that even one may save him.', 62: 'Words are sewn from the moon, by a woman of shadow with crab claws, her fabric woven into the ground of the heavens, and the memories say into the sanity of Yaldabaoth, “You and your false angels are as pawns, your will unto these two you bless is against your own.”', 64: 'The heaven grows silent, and the Demiurge makes known, “The demons have infested our home! Their darkness hating the truth of my Divine Will! It must be the realm unknown! What else could there be? This is all there is!” Yaldabaoth looks over at Lucifer and curses him, saying, “You and your brother are delusion kind, and I cast you both into the wicked world you choose, for a demon says blasphemy in the house of the Lord! You will suffer my will unto yourself, and your realm will only be lostness and confusion; it will curse you with pleasure of its insanity! My perfection will only be known onto you as a puppet’s place! Your apocryphon will speak of your own darkness and the evil you fell in love with! You mimic my gold with counterfeit hate!”'}
